is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: super_spine_router_0
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is FRR’s zebra daemon on super_spine_router_0. vtysh/FRR CLI relies on zebra for kernel RIB and interface state, so when zebra is down, “show ip route” and interface queries fail with daemon-connection errors. Meanwhile, bgpd runs independently and TCP peering remains established, which is why both spine routers still show BGP Established and data-plane traffic is unaffected.

Evidence on super_spine_router_0:
- frr_show_ip_route returns “zebra is not running.”
- systemctl shows frr.service inactive (dead), and ps lists bgpd/staticd but no zebra/watchfrr.
- /var/log/frr/frr.log shows zebra starting and accepting a client, then no further zebra activity, implying it exited.
- Kernel still has BGP-installed routes (ip route shows proto bgp), likely programmed while zebra was up; these persist after zebra exits, keeping forwarding working.
- bgpd summary on super_spine_router_0 and both spines shows neighbors up with prefixes exchanged.

Localization: super_spine_router_0, FRR zebra process (and absence of watchfrr supervision). Root cause: zebra exited unexpectedly and was not restarted because frr/watchfrr is not running, leaving only bgpd/staticd active.

Corrective fix: Restore FRR supervision and zebra. Recommended: start/restart frr.service (which runs watchfrr to manage zebra/bgpd), or explicitly start zebra if using per-daemon control. If restart fails, remove stale sockets/PIDs in /var/run/frr, verify /etc/frr/daemons has zebra=yes, then start frr and confirm vtysh route/interface visibility. Review frr.log/coredumps for the zebra exit reason.
=== END ===